<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/intermediate/optics-lenses-and-mirrors">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Light bends when it changes speed — at every glass surface. A curved lens or mirror uses
    that bending to reunite rays, and one thin-lens equation organises all of it:
    <strong>1/f = 1/do + 1/di</strong>, where f is the focal length, do the object distance and
    di the image distance. Everything about cameras, eyes, microscopes and telescopes follows
    from that one relation plus the two ray rules (angle of reflection equals angle of
    incidence; Snell's bending at every refraction). This chapter is geometric optics: treat
    light as rays, and the whole industry of image-making becomes algebra and two ruled lines —
    no smoke, no mirrors, except where the mirrors are the point.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the lens equation as a curve — as the object approaches the focal point, the image runs away to infinity" widget="plotter" config={lensCfg} />

  <p>
    Read the curve like a photographer. Object far away → image lands just past f (the sensor
    plane). Object at 2f → image at 2f, same size (the "1:1" macro case). Object slides toward
    f = 10 cm → image distance explodes — that's the projector regime, throwing a giant image
    forward. Inside f the maths flips sign: the image is virtual, upright, magnified — a
    magnifying glass.
  </p>

  <h2>Reflection: mirrors and images</h2>
  <p>
    The law of reflection — <strong>angle of incidence equals angle of reflection</strong>,
    measured from the surface normal — looks trivial and is not: it is time-reversibility in
    action, and it alone explains the plane mirror. The image you see in a bathroom mirror is
    <strong>virtual</strong> (the rays do not actually pass through it; your brain extends
    them backwards), upright, the same size, and exactly as far behind the mirror as you are
    in front — checkable with a friend and a rangefinder, or the classic "how big must a
    mirror be to see your whole body?" answer: half your height, wherever it hangs.
    <strong>Curved mirrors</strong> scale the same law up: a concave mirror (like a spoon's
    bowl, or a shaving mirror) converges parallel rays to a real focal point and can form
    real, inverted images (projector headlights, telescope mirrors, solar furnaces); a convex
    mirror diverges rays and gives small, upright, wide-angle virtual images — the security
    mirror's whole job is that shrunk field of view. Rough surfaces scatter the law in a
    million directions (diffuse reflection) — which is why paper and walls are visible from
    any angle while mirrors show you one.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> mirrors reverse left and right. They actually reverse
  <em>front–back</em> (the axis toward the mirror). Text in a mirror reads reversed because
  <em>you</em> turned it around to face the mirror; stand on the far side of a sheet of
  glass and your twin would find your "left-right" unswapped. Mirror writing is a
  coordination trick, not an optical one.</p>

  <h2>Refraction: light changes speed, so it changes direction</h2>
  <p>
    Light travels slower in matter — glass about ⅔ of c, water about ¾ — and the ratio
    c/v defines the <strong>refractive index</strong> n (air ≈ 1.0003, water 1.33, glass
    ≈ 1.5, diamond 2.42). At a boundary hit at an angle, the wavefront's edge arrives
    early and pivots: light entering a denser medium bends <strong>toward the normal</strong>,
    exiting it bends away — <strong>Snell's law</strong>, n₁sin θ₁ = n₂sin θ₂. This is why a
    straw looks broken at the waterline, why pools are shallower than they look (light from
    the floor refracts away on exit, so the floor's image floats up — a 2 m-deep pool looks
    about 1.5 m deep), and why spearfishing requires aiming below the fish.
  </p>
  <p>
    Push the geometry the other way — light going from dense to sparse — and past a certain
    angle it cannot exit at all: it reflects <em>totally</em>. The
    <strong>critical angle</strong> is arcsin(n₂/n₁): about 48.8° for water-to-air,
    42° for glass-to-air. <strong>Total internal reflection</strong> is the working principle
    of optical fibres (light bouncing down a hair-thin glass core for kilometres with almost
    no loss — the internet's physical backbone, and endoscopes), of prisms in binoculars
    (folding the optical path), and of the diamond's fire (its huge n makes the critical
    angle tiny, so light entering a cut stone rattles around inside until it exits toward
    your eye).
  </p>

  <h2>Thin lenses: the equation and the images</h2>
  <p>
    A lens is refraction twice — once entering, once leaving a curved surface — and a thin
    lens folds the pair into one focal length f. <strong>Converging (convex)</strong> lenses
    focus parallel rays to a real focus; <strong>diverging (concave)</strong> lenses spread
    them as if from a virtual focus. Ray-tracing rules make any case drawable in seconds:
    a ray parallel to the axis passes through the focus; a ray through the centre passes
    straight on; a ray through the focus exits parallel. The lens equation
    1/f = 1/do + 1/di then locates the image, with magnification m = −di/do (the minus sign
    flagging inversion). Worked example: an object 30 cm from a converging lens of f = 10 cm:
    1/di = 1/10 − 1/30 = 2/30, so di = <strong>15 cm</strong> — real, on the far side — and
    m = −15/30 = <strong>−0.5</strong>: inverted, half-size, exactly a camera's working
    regime. Object inside f (do = 5 cm): 1/di = 1/10 − 1/5 = −1/10, so di = −10 cm — a
    negative sign meaning <em>virtual</em>: upright and magnified (m = +2), the magnifying
    glass. Diverging lenses always give the small-upright-virtual kind, which is why the
    peephole in a door and a myopic person's glasses both show a shrunken world.
  </p>

  <h2>The eye: a self-focusing camera</h2>
  <p>
    Your eye is a liquid-filled lens of about 17 mm focal length whose <strong>cornea does
    roughly two-thirds of the bending</strong> (air-to-fluid is the biggest index jump you
    own) and whose crystalline lens fine-focuses by changing shape — fat for near objects,
    flat for far ones (<strong>accommodation</strong>). Underwater, the cornea's air
    interface vanishes and the eye nearly stops focusing: why swimming is blurry and goggles
    restore the air gap. Vision errors are focal-length errors: in <strong>short
    sight</strong> (myopia) the eyeball is too long and distant images focus in front of the
    retina — fixed with a diverging lens; in <strong>long sight</strong> (hyperopia),
    converging lenses help. Lens strength is quoted in <strong>dioptres</strong> (power =
    1/f in metres; a +2.0 D lens has f = 0.5 m), and reading-glasses strengths are just the
    top of that scale. The retina's rods and cones, colour and night vision, belong to the
    perception lessons; the optics stops here.
  </p>

  <h2>Dispersion: why prisms and rainbows</h2>
  <p>
    The refractive index is slightly different for each colour — violet slows (and bends)
    more than red in glass. One prism therefore fans white light into its spectrum; one
    <strong>rainbow</strong> is a billion raindrops doing the same job, each drop refracting
    sunlight, reflecting it internally once, and refracting it again, sending violet (which
    exits at about 40°) and red (about 42°) to your eye in concentric arcs — with the
    secondary rainbow outside, its colours reversed, from two internal reflections. The same
    wavelength dependence is a lens designer's headache: simple lenses focus violet and red
    at slightly different distances (<strong>chromatic aberration</strong> — the purple
    fringe on cheap optics), cured by stacking a converging and diverging lens of different
    glasses into an achromatic doublet, invented in the 1730s and in every camera since.
  </p>

  <h2>The camera: the eye's mechanical twin</h2>
  <p>
    A camera fixes the problems the eye solves biologically. Focusing is mechanical — the
    lens (or sensor) moves, since glass cannot squash. Exposure trades two dials:
    <strong>aperture</strong> (quoted as f-numbers — f/2 means the diameter is half the focal
    length; halving the f-number quadruples the light-gathering area) and shutter speed.
    Depth of field follows from aperture: wide open (f/1.8) throws backgrounds into creamy
    blur, stopped down (f/11) keeps nearly everything sharp — because a smaller aperture
    tolerates bigger focusing errors before circles of confusion grow. Smartphone cameras
    "cheat" the physics their thin bodies impose: tiny sensors, then software (computational
    photography, multi-frame stacking, AI depth maps) rebuilding the depth of field that a
    larger lens would have delivered. The optics is unchanged; the compromises moved to the
    algorithm — but the lens equation still runs underneath every shot.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Telescopes: the aperture is the argument.</strong> A telescope's magnification
    is trivially adjustable (swap eyepieces); what cannot be cheated is <strong>light-gathering
    area</strong> — the pupil's 5 mm versus a 10 m mirror's millionfold advantage is why the
    faint universe opens only to big glass. Refractors (lenses) suffer chromatic aberration
    and sag under their own weight, so every giant telescope since 1900 is a reflector —
    mirrors can be supported from behind — and modern designs go further: segmented mirrors,
    active optics correcting sag in real time, and adaptive optics that deform a mirror
    hundreds of times a second to cancel atmospheric shimmer using a laser "guide star".
    The largest single mirrors now exceed 10 m; the James Webb Space Telescope's 6.5 m
    segmented beryllium mirror works in infrared from beyond the Moon.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Why Snell's law is Snell's law: Fermat's principle.</strong> The deepest
    justification is a principle, not a mechanism: light takes the path of <em>least
    time</em>. Between two points across a boundary, the fastest route is not the straight
    one — it angles to spend more distance in the fast medium, exactly as a lifeguard runs
    along the beach before swimming. Minimising the time reproduces Snell's law precisely,
    and the same principle generates the law of reflection. Fermat's least-time (and its
    quantum descendent, Feynman's sum-over-paths) is one of those junctures where physics
    reveals its economy: the rules of lenses are consequences of a single optimisation
    principle.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>The diffraction ceiling.</strong> Geometric optics pretends rays are
    infinitely thin, but light is a wave (the Waves chapter), and a wave passing any
    aperture spreads. A lens's resolution is therefore limited by diffraction: the finest
    detail a perfect lens of diameter D can resolve scales with λ/D. For your pupil at
    night (7 mm) and 550 nm light, that's about 0.1 mrad — matching the eye's real
    acuity, no coincidence; evolution built the retina up to its optics' limit. The same
    ceiling sets microscope objectives' numerical apertures, telescope angular resolution
    (why radio arrays spread dishes across continents), and the end of classical
    microscopes — seeing atoms needed electrons, whose wavelengths are thousands of times
    shorter. Every optical instrument you will ever design lives under λ/D.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Frontier optics.</strong> Metamaterials with engineered refractive indices below
    1 (and even negative, in the lab) bend light the "wrong" way and have demonstrated
    rudimentary cloaking at microwave frequencies; gradient-index lenses built into
    fibre-optic collimators and animal eyes (fish lenses!) do focusing with no surfaces at
    all; and adaptive contact lenses aim to switch a wearer's focus electronically. The
    400-year-old thin-lens equation still runs the first approximation of every one of
    these — the difference between a good physicist and a great one is knowing exactly
    where it stops being true.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Snell (1621); Fermat (1662); OpenStax, College Physics (ch. 25–26, Geometric Optics); Hecht, Optics; JWST/NASA optics documentation.</p>
</TopicPage>
